---
/**
 * /careers — the company and every open role, on one page.
 *
 * This was two pages: /resources/hiring ("Work at Mersive" — offices, process,
 * benefits, EEO) and /careers (the listing). They are merged here, and /careers is
 * the survivor for two reasons: every job posting already lives beneath it at
 * /careers/<slug>, so retiring it would orphan the postings, and it is the URL a
 * candidate and a search engine both expect. /resources/hiring now redirects here.
 *
 * The order is a candidate's order, not the org chart's: what the company is, then
 * what is open, then how applying works, then where the work happens and what comes
 * with it. The roles sit high because someone who arrived to job-hunt should not
 * have to scroll past our culture copy to find them.
 *
 * Roles are read from the Rippling board at build time (src/data/jobs.ts), grouped
 * by department, one card each, each linking to its own page. Applying happens in
 * Rippling and nowhere else: no form here, and no candidate data crosses
 * mersive.com. The roster is only as current as the last deploy — a role filled
 * today stays listed until the next push.
 *
 * The copy says nothing about where the roles come from or where an application
 * goes: the Apply button on each posting does that, and narrating it to a candidate
 * adds nothing they need.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import JobCard from "../../components/JobCard.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import {
  JOBS,
  JOBS_BY_DEPARTMENT,
  JOBS_REACHABLE,
  JOB_BOARD_URL,
  jobCountLabel,
} from "../../data/jobs";

/* Three states, not two. An unreachable listing must not claim nothing is open —
   we do not know that, and telling a candidate there are no roles when there may be
   several is the one wrong answer this page can give. */
const rolesHead =
  JOBS.length > 0
    ? `${jobCountLabel()} right now.`
    : JOBS_REACHABLE
      ? "Nothing is open today."
      : "The listing could not be loaded.";
const rolesDek =
  JOBS.length > 0
    ? "Every role we are hiring for, each with its full description on this site, grouped by the team it sits in."
    : JOBS_REACHABLE
      ? "Rather than leave roles up after they are filled, this page shows nothing when nothing is open. New roles appear as soon as they are posted."
      : "That is a fault on our side rather than a sign that nothing is open. The link below always shows every current opening.";
---

<BaseLayout route="careers">
  <PageIntro
    route={"careers"}
    tpl={"T8"}
    open={false}
    gate={"GATED: HR CONTENT OWED"}
    crumb={"Careers"}
    section={false}
    title={"Work at Mersive."}
  />
  <p class="dek">
    One product, two offices, and a company owned by the people who design, engineer, sell and
    support it. That ownership is not a slogan on this page: it is why the people who answer a
    support ticket and the people who decide what ships are in the same building.
  </p>

  <div class="cnv-band reveal">
    <div class="kicker">Open roles</div>
    <h2>{rolesHead}</h2>
    <p>{rolesDek}</p>
    {
      JOBS.length === 0 && (
        <p style="margin:0">
          <a class="btn accent big" href={JOB_BOARD_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            View all openings ↗
          </a>
        </p>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* The full listing, not a featured handful. The merged page has no second page
       to send anyone to, so every open role has to be reachable from right here. */
    JOBS_BY_DEPARTMENT.map((group) => (
      <section class="sect reveal flush">
        <div class="kicker">{group.department}</div>
        <h2>
          {group.jobs.length} {group.jobs.length === 1 ? "role" : "roles"}
        </h2>
        <div class="job-list">
          {group.jobs.map((job) => (
            <JobCard job={job} hideDepartment />
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">Speculative applications</div>
    <h2>Nothing that fits?</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Send it anyway if you build the kind of thing this company builds. Where a speculative
      application should go, and who reads it, is not settled. [general-application route - HR]
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">How to apply</div>
    <h2>Apply once, to a person.</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Every step below is owed by HR before this page publishes. What is written here is the sequence
      the page will describe, not a commitment about timing or stages.
    </p>
    <div class="three">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>1 &middot; Apply</h3>
        <p>Find the role above and apply against it. [required materials per role - HR]</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>2 &middot; Interview</h3>
        <p>[interview process - HR] Stages, who you meet, and how long it takes, stated plainly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>3 &middot; Offer</h3>
        <p>
          [offer and onboarding steps - HR] Compensation ranges are not published on this page and no
          figure here is confirmed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">Where the work happens</div>
    <h2>Two offices.</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Both are real places with people in them. Which roles are on-site, hybrid, or remote is set per
      role in the posting. [remote and hybrid policy per role - HR]
    </p>
    <div class="two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Headquarters &middot; Lakewood, Colorado</h3>
        <p>1667 Cole Blvd, Suite 225<br />Lakewood, CO 80401</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>EMEA &middot; Pozna&#324;, Poland</h3>
        <p>
          The EMEA office. [Pozna&#324; street address: not published anywhere on the site today -
          HR and facilities]
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Both offices already appear on <a href="/contact">the contact page</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">Benefits</div>
    <h2>What comes with the job.</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Benefits differ by country and are not the same in Lakewood and Pozna&#324;, so this section
      publishes as two lists rather than one averaged claim.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      [benefits summary - HR] Nothing is listed until HR supplies it per location. Inventing a
      benefits list is a promise to a candidate that the company has not made.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="cnv-band reveal">
    <div class="kicker">Equal opportunity</div>
    <h2>The statement goes here, in full.</h2>
    <p class="note" style="margin:0">
      [EEO statement: final wording - HR and legal] This is a legal statement, not marketing copy: it
      publishes verbatim as counsel and HR write it, and nothing stands in for it in the meantime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Who you would be joining</h3>
      <p>Owned by the people who build Polaris, and what that changes about how the company runs.</p>
      <a href="/resources/who">Who we are →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>What you would be working on</h3>
      <p>The platform, the workspace, and fifteen years of it: from Sol to Polaris.</p>
      <a href="/platform/lineage">The platform story →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <CtaBand variant="story" />
</BaseLayout>
